[#:seon.task{:name "reset-takes-over-six-seconds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "bin/seon --root R reset NAME --force took 6.4-12.7 s on a fresh disposable root (stop and unlink under 0.2 s): almost all of it start! re-standing the cluster in the same JVM. Profiled (8.4 s with 63 hosts): `seon.cluster/arm-host-program!` re-armed all 2,584 contracted Vars (2.2 s) because seon.instrument's current-wrapper? compared the call-preparation snapshot's checked-through-t, a database position no wrapper reads; `seon.fn.analyzer/ensure-dependency-analysis!` linted the checkout's non-program roots (1.2 s); the new cluster's base-context derivation (1.1 s, cold index nodes); stopping 63 hosts (1.7 s). Correct: reset reuses the JVM's armed program, loaded namespaces and caches so it costs well under 2 s. Proof: seon.instrument-test/a-later-database-value-of-the-same-program-keeps-every-wrapper, plus profile evidence of start! after a reset."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.boot/start! seon.cluster/arm-host-program! seon.fn.analyzer/ensure-dependency-analysis!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/host-cost/host-cost-notes.md"}}

 #:seon.task{:name "a-development-cluster-boots-in-tens-of-seconds"
  :kind :function
  :intent "`seon.sci.eval-instrumentation-test/an-instrumented-dev-cluster-builds-an-attempt-ready-prompt` boots one real development cluster with `seon.cluster.boot/start!` and ran 49.9 s (declared bound 5 s; throwaway JVM check on 7a9ceb44a with two other check JVMs running). Over ten seconds needs an issue: profile start! on an empty store, name the armed functions that dominate and fix them. Done when the same test measures under its declared bound."
  :must-read #{seon.cluster.boot/start!}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/review/red-triage.md"}}

 #:seon.task{:name "one-cluster-without-a-database-fails-the-whole-status"
  :kind :function
  :intent "`bin/seon status` failed whole (exit 1) on default at 17:35 with `seon.health/render-ai refused argument 0 at [:seon.db/db]` from `seon.cluster.boot/request!`'s `:status` branch, and answered OK on the next call: one running instance had no store connection at that moment (a start or stop in the same JVM), so `request!` built the render unit without `:seon.db/db`, while `render-ai` already handles an absent database (`(if database ...)`) but its contract `:seon.render/unit` requires it. One cluster's missing database must neither fail the other clusters' rows nor the status: fix the contract or the caller at the producer, and render that instance's row as its readiness. Proof: a status request with one instance lacking `:seon.boot/cluster-connection` answers every row, the incomplete one as data."
  :must-read #{seon.cluster.boot/request! seon.health/render-ai}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/model-pass/base-roster.md"}}]
